 was threatening hxg5, winning a bishop. The bishop steps away.","White answers 26.g4, pushing the pawn storm towards the king.","After 26.g4 Qc7 27.Bd2 Qe7 28.gxf5 Qxh4 29.Qxh4 Bxh4 30.fxe6 Bxe6 31.Bg6, the queens come off (+0.15)."]},{"ply":54,"name":"27…fxg4","cls":"blunder","color":"b","headline":"27…fxg4 is a blunder: Black's winning chances go from 48% to 12%.","points":["White answers 28.Bg6.","After Bg6, White wins 1 point of material within 4 moves.","Better was 27…Qb3 (+0.19)."],"better":"27…Qb3 threatens …Qd1+, winning two pawns, picking it up with 29…Qxg4+. Engine line: 28.Bc2 Qc4 29.h5 Bg5 30.gxf5 exf5 31.Qd2 (+0.19)."},{"ply":55,"name":"28.Bg6","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"28.Bg6! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["The next best move, 28.b4, would drop White's winning chances from 88% to 52% (+0.22).","28.Bg6 wins a bishop, picking it up with 29.Bxa5, and threatens Qf7+, with mate in 2.","Sets up a sequence and wins a bishop once the captures settle, picking it up with 29.Bxa5.","It threatens Qf7+, with mate in 2, so Black has to spend the next move on it.","Black answers 28…Qc7.","After 28…Qc7 29.Bxa5 Bxh4 30.Bxc7 Bxf2+ 31.Kxf2 Bd7, White comes out a piece up (+5.41)."]},{"ply":57,"name":"29.Bxa5","cls":"brilliant","color":"w","headline":"29.Bxa5!! A real sacrifice: White gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Puts the bishop on a5 where it can be taken.","Taking is too dangerous: Black's best reply is 29…Bxh4 30.Bxc7 Bxf2+ 31.Kxf2 instead (the engine rates it +5.89).","Takes the pawn on a5.","Leaves Black with an isolated pawn on b7: a long-term weakness.","29.Bxa5 wins a bishop and threatens Bxc7, with mate in 5.","Takes the a5 pawn and wins a bishop once the captures settle."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"29.Bxa5!! offers the bishop on a5. Taking it leads to mate."},{"title":"What White gives up","text":"29.Bxa5 puts the bishop on a5 where it can be taken by the queen on c7. That's a bishop on offer."},{"title":"If Black takes the bishop","text":"29…Qxa5 is met by 30.Qf7+. Then 31.Qe8# is mate."},{"title":"Black's best try","text":"Black's best is to leave the bishop alone and play 29…Bxh4. White answers 30.Bxc7, taking the queen on c7 and threatening Qf7+ and mate. Then 31.Kxf2 takes the bishop on f2, and 33.a4 attacks the bishop on b5, which is undefended. White ends up a piece ahead: White is winning (+5.89)."},{"title":"Without the sacrifice","text":"The best move without the sacrifice, 29.b4, is clearly weaker (+3.74 against +5.89). Black answers 29…Qd7, preparing …Be7 and …Kh8. White would still be winning, but far less than after 29.Bxa5."}]},{"ply":59,"name":"30.Qf1","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"30.Qf1! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Moves the attacked queen to safety.","The next best move, 30.Qf4, would drop White's winning chances from 99% to 88% (+5.54).","30.Qf1 gets the queen out of the way of …gxf2+.","Black was threatening …gxf2+, winning the queen and a bishop. The queen steps away.","Black answers 30…b6, attacking the bishop on a5, which is undefended.","After 30…b6 31.Bb4 Bf6 32.exf6 Qf7 33.Bxf7+ Kxf7 34.fxg7+ Kxg7 35.Qf8+, White comes out a queen's worth up (+14.19)."]}],"opening":{"name":"Slav Defense: Exchange Variation","eco":"D13","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 8.e3: 15 moves in all, 8 by White and 7 by Black.","Theory runs out after 8.e3: too few games between strong players reached this position to go further."]}}